# Experimental Integration: Gemma 4

I successfully scanned the `models\Gemm4` directory. You have provided a GGUF quantized variant of the Gemma 4 instruct model:
`gemma-4-E4B-it-Q4_K_M.gguf`

The fantastic news is that because it is a `.gguf` file, it is automatically compatible with our underlying `llama_cpp` architecture. However, integrating this model requires two critical modifications, and one major hardware warning.

## Hardware Warning (VRAM Overflow)
> [!CAUTION]
> Your `1050 Ti` has a hard physiological limit of **4.0 GB** of VRAM. 
> The `gemma` file you downloaded weighs **5.3 GB**.
> 
> You cannot cram 5.3 GB of data into a 4.0 GB pipe. When we try to load this, the engine will either shatter via a CUDA Out of Memory (OOM) crash, or our safety fallback will catch it and offload half the model into your System CPU RAM. 
> If it spills into your System RAM, the processing speed will dynamically plummet. Are you prepared to accept significantly slower translation speeds to test this model?

## Proposed Changes

### [MODIFY] [translate.py](file:///c:/just-dub-it2/app/stages/translate.py)

1. **Model Swap**: Update the `load_llm()` logic to target the massive `gemma-4-E4B-it-Q4_K_M.gguf` file instead of Llama.
2. **Dialect Token Restructuring**: Gemma models speak an entirely different language underneath the hood than Llama models. If we feed Gemma our current Llama prompt, it will violently hallucinate. We must swap out the prompt tags:
   - *Llama tags:* `<|eot_id|>`
   - *Gemma tags:* `<start_of_turn>` and `<end_of_turn>`
   
We will rewrite the prompt logic exclusively for the Gemma format to maintain the dialect limits and time-stretching protections we established earlier today.

## User Action Required
Please confirm that you understand the 5.3GB > 4.0GB VRAM overflow bottleneck. If you approve, I will strip out Llama 3, inject Gemma 4, format the prompt, and reboot the system.
